1. Before Java 8: Interfaces could only declare abstract methods

    Suppose Java had this interface:

        interface Payment {
            void pay();
        }

    Many developers create their own classes implementing it:

        class CreditCard implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using credit card");
            }
        }

        class UPI implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using UPI");
            }
    }

Everything works.

2. Now imagine Java adds a new method

    Suppose Java designers decide:

    Every payment method should also support refunds.

    They change the interface:

        interface Payment {
            void pay();
            void refund();  // NEW METHOD
        }

    What happens?

    Your existing classes:

        class CreditCard implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using credit card");
            }
        }

    Now produce an error:

    CreditCard is not abstract and does not override abstract method refund()

    Why?

    Because CreditCard promised:

    I implement the Payment interface.

    But now Payment has two required methods, and CreditCard only implements one.

    Every existing implementation must be modified:

        class CreditCard implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using credit card");
            }

            public void refund() {
                System.out.println("Refund processed");
            }
        }

    Imagine thousands of classes across the world implementing a popular interface. Adding one method becomes dangerous.

3. Java 8 solution: Default method

    Instead of adding an abstract method: void refund();

    Java allows:

        default void refund() {
            System.out.println("Default refund process");
        }

    Complete interface:

        interface Payment {
            void pay();

            default void refund() {
                System.out.println("Default refund process");
            }
        }

    Now old classes do not break:

        class CreditCard implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using credit card");
            }
        }

    This is valid because refund() already has an implementation in the interface.

    CreditCard c = new CreditCard();
    c.pay();
    c.refund();

    Output:

    Paid using credit card
    Default refund process

4. A class can override the default method if needed

        class UPI implements Payment {
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Paid using UPI");
            }

            @Override
            public void refund() {
                System.out.println("UPI-specific refund");
            }
        }

    Now:

    Payment p = new UPI();
    p.refund();

    Output:

    UPI-specific refund

    So:

    Default method = optional override, because the interface already provides an implementation.

5. Relating this to forEach()

Before Java 8, imagine:

    interface Collection {
        int size();
        boolean add(Object obj);
        // etc.
    }

    Thousands of classes implement Collection:

    ArrayList
    LinkedList
    HashSet
    Custom collection classes written by developers

    Java wanted to introduce: void forEach(...)

    If they added it as abstract: void forEach(...);

    then every existing collection implementation would need to implement it.

    Instead, Java 8 added a default implementation conceptually like:

    default void forEach(Consumer action) {
        for (Object obj : this) {
            action.accept(obj);
        }
    }

    Therefore:

    ArrayList
    LinkedList
    HashSet

    could automatically use the new method without changing their source code.

    Example:

    List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

    numbers.add(10);
    numbers.add(20);
    numbers.add(30);

    numbers.forEach(n -> System.out.println(n));

    ArrayList does not necessarily need to individually implement forEach() just because the interface gained it. It can inherit the default implementation.

    The key difference

    Abstract method:

    Every implementing class MUST provide its own implementation.

    Default method:

    The interface provides an implementation. Implementing classes MAY override it.

    interface A {
        void test();  // Must override

        default void show() {  // Already implemented
            System.out.println("Hello");
        }
    }

    Default methods were introduced in Java 8 to allow interfaces to evolve by adding new methods with implementations, without forcing all existing implementing classes to override those methods.